A||N||6||Formula88||No Power - Suggestions?||||5||
Z||000000||Formula88||05-12-2001||05:47 PM||||Hey, guys.  Yes I did a search, but here's my question.<P>'88 Formula, 5-speed, all stock 2.8.  New clutch (about 5k miles ago).  The car runs smooth, no apparent spark knock or misfire, but has no power.  When I first got the car on the road with the new clutch it had lots of power.  On one stretch of road, after coming off my favorite freeway offramp, I could just touch 100mph before slowing down.  Now, I'm hard pressed to reach 75-80.  I can put it in first and stand on it, and it'll pull (slowly) up to about 4700rpm and then just sit there like it hit a rev limiter.  Also, it used to bark 2nd real good on a power shift, but won't do it now no matter what.  It has a new cap, rotor, spark plugs and ignition module (ouch! those are expensive!), and I sprayed most of a can of carb&fuel inj cleaner into the plenum to clean out any buildup.  I don't have a timing light yet, so I'll have to borrow one to verify the timing.  It hasn't been changed, so unless something came loose or shifted, it should be ok. My first concern was that my clutch might be slipping, but that doesn't appear to be the problem since the revs climb slower than before.  Any ideas on where to start?  <P>Thought about a plugged cat, but there's a recp't from the guy I bought the car from for a replacement, and it just passed an emissions inspection last month, so the cat may be ok. How should I check it?<P>Oh, yea, and no codes from the ECM.  The engine runs smooth, but has no power.  I'd be afraid of an Iron Duke automatic right now!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||hugh||05-12-2001||09:46 PM||||Check your fuel pump/filter.I'm not familiar with stock Fieros,so I can't tell you where you can check your fuel pressure,or where the filter may be located.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/hugh_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||sjp777||05-12-2001||09:52 PM||||I would still say plugged cat. They can go at anytime. If it's not so bad when it's cold, that would be a good clue. The only way to really check is to yank it and throw a straight pipe on. If the prob goes away, you have your answer.<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula88||05-13-2001||04:18 AM||||Fuel filter was changed a month ago, and pressure checked ok at that time - but it could have gotten clogged.  Yes, the problem is less severe when the car's cold, so I think I'll yank the exhaust and see.  If it's just a clogged cat, that will be an easy fix.  Here's hoping!  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroPerformance||05-15-2001||10:05 PM||||Try checking the ignition coil in the distributer and make sure that non of your plug wires are shorting out on exhaust manifolds.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Formula88||05-15-2001||10:18 PM||||Well, I haven't had a chance to check, but I'm pretty sure it's an exhaust restriction (cat clogged?).  Under heavy throttle, it starts sounding like someone blowing through a straw, and I got the faint red glowing exhaust manifolds after a hard test drive.  It's getting worse, so these symptoms have just surfaced.  Now I just gotta get the time to crawl under and check the exhaust.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Seanh||05-15-2001||10:35 PM||||Sounds very much like a clogged cat. Tap on the bottem of it with a screw driver or somthing and listen to see if you can hear anything shakeing around in there.||||reg||1
